WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.090
Meet Xiao Zhu, a fictional patient, collecting a repeat prescription.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.500
For one medicine, the pharmacy's screen opens his whole record.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.690
MedSSI's 2025 story page, re-run on a laptop in 2026.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:22.970
The pharmacist needs the medicine and dose, the days' supply, and an allergy check.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.510
Not his diagnoses, his psychiatric notes, or his ID number.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:35.830
MedSSI's idea: the hospital issues a digital credential once, into the government wallet app on his phone.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.750
Issuing and signature checks ran on the Ministry of Digital Affairs' test sandbox.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:50.630
In the team's 2025 recording, the sandbox answers an allergy-card request with a real one-time QR code.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.678
The turn: selective disclosure.

00:00:53.678 --> 00:00:59.160
The pharmacy asks only for the pickup fields; his wallet shows what will be shared.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.610
He agrees, and only those go.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:06.100
MedSSI's server check rejects any field that wasn't requested.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.150
The story page spells it out: what is shown, and what is not.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:16.838
On the checking side, the sandbox returned a request QR for one scope.

00:01:16.838 --> 00:01:18.780
The phone scan wasn't filmed.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.952
Share the prescription, not the record.

00:01:22.952 --> 00:01:28.820
MedSSI was a 2025 student prototype on a test sandbox, not a medical product.
